// Lesson 5.2 — The Door: Opening It with Events
// Lever.Interact (REAL shipping code) + the E-key interaction the student builds,
// and UnityEvents as the abstraction between cause and effect.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (verified by reading the project and the Overworld scene YAML):
//   - Lever.cs IS real working code. offSprite/onSprite are assigned; Interact()
//     is `public`; its two UnityEvent fields are `private` AND [SerializeField].
//     That `private` is load-bearing teaching: a subscriber in C# CANNOT call
//     onLeverOn.AddListener(...) until the field is exposed, because serialization
//     reaches a private field and C# does not. The ONLY out-of-the-box route is
//     the Inspector. Walkthrough 2 makes exposing it the one legitimate edit to
//     somebody else's file, with the reason attached.
//   - The scene's switch object is named CaveSwitch. Its onLeverOn has exactly
//     THREE persistent listeners, all calling SetActive with a bool argument,
//     targeting in order: "Collision Door Removed", "No Collision Door Removed",
//     and the scene's unused "Canvas". onLeverOff has ZERO listeners. These are
//     PREFAB-INSTANCE OVERRIDES on the scene copy — the prefab's own arrays are
//     empty, so the student must select the object in the SCENE. (The serialized
//     array still carries two stale null target entries from when it was shrunk;
//     they target nothing and never fire.)
//   - So the door mechanism ALREADY WORKS. The student's job is the E-key
//     interaction, then reading/extending somebody else's wiring.
//   - PlayerController.cs is an empty stub - the E-key scan does NOT exist. The
//     student builds it. Project is Unity 6 (6000.6.0f1), legacy Input class:
//     Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.E) is correct. FindObjectOfType<T>() is deprecated
//     in Unity 6 - teach FindFirstObjectByType<T>().
//
// SHAPE (rewritten from the old API-listing + question-only Build Lab):
//   Scaffolding is LEVEL 1 - FULL WALKTHROUGH, and asks.ts agrees. C# events are
//   the first mechanism in this course whose "part" has no name to search for
//   (`Invoke()` calls nobody), so the lesson is the full treatment: zero gaps in
//   EITHER walkthrough, and all six decompose parts given with `does` + `doc`.
//
//   - renderDecompose('5.2', ..., 1) opens the body, like 4.1. Six parts in two
//     halves: reaching the lever (1-4) and the event that connects it to the door
//     (5-6). Every part has a reading; none is blank.
//   - TWO renderWalkthrough calls, both level 1 (a level-1 lesson gives every
//     line, and a lesson's walkthroughs must declare the same rung):
//       WALK_INTERACT  PlayerController.cs  - the single-frame key check, the
//                      Unity 6 scene search, the null guard, the distance, the
//                      call to the public Interact().
//       WALK_SUBSCRIBE DoorOpener.cs        - the subscriber BUILT in code:
//                      expose the private event, AddListener in Start(), the
//                      handler, and RemoveListener in OnDestroy(). It plants the
//                      idea 5.3 pays off: things that survive a scene change must
//                      be cleaned up.
//     Walkthrough 2 is the ALTERNATIVE road. The Inspector route from the old
//     "spend the wiring you inherited" step is kept in full - it is the exam's
//     abstraction point and the only route that works without editing Lever.cs.
//   - Both finalFiles are deliberately a different-but-equivalent idiom from
//     their own steps (one folded `&&` condition instead of two early returns;
//     the delegate held in a UnityAction field instead of a method group passed
//     twice), so comparing files is reading for meaning, not diffing text.
//     Nothing a later lesson depends on is renamed.
//
// PROTECTED BLOCKS - ids are POSITIONAL, so nothing here is reordered, renumbered
// or re-counted. All five keep their document position and their item counts:
//   socratic  "Where does the game's knowledge live?"   4 items + no table
//   socratic  "Three ways to decide am I near a lever?" 4 items + its 3-row table
//   bug-hunt  "The event that wouldn't"                 5 items
//   tinker    "Event design"                            4 items
//   checklist                                           6 items
// Only individual wording changed, where the new prose would otherwise have
