        <em>in the scene</em> — the object standing in the Hierarchy, not the prefab in the Project
        window. Look in its Inspector under <strong>On Lever On</strong>. This is the part that
        should surprise you: <strong>somebody has already wired it up.</strong> Three listeners are
        waiting there, and each one calls <code>SetActive</code> with a true/false argument. Before
        you read on, expand each one and find out what it targets.
    </p>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Select the object in the scene, not the prefab.</strong> Those three listeners are
        <em>prefab-instance overrides</em>: they were added to the copy of <code>CaveSwitch</code>
        that lives in the Overworld, so the prefab asset in the Project window still has two empty
        event lists. That is normal Unity behaviour and worth knowing — a prefab is a starting
        point, and an instance drifts from it on purpose. If you inspect the prefab and see nothing
        wired, nothing is wrong; you are looking at the wrong object.
    </div>

    <p>
        Two of the listeners target objects called <code>Collision Door Removed</code> and
        <code>No Collision Door Removed</code>. Find them in the Hierarchy, then look at them in
        the scene. Together they <em>are</em> the door: one object for the wall you can feel, one
