            <li>If you chose search-and-measure, remove the distance check and press E from across the map. What did that check protect you from?</li>
            <li>Wire the door's <code>SetActive(false)</code> into <strong>On Lever Off</strong> instead of On. Now what is the exact key-press sequence to get through? Is that a puzzle or a bug?</li>
            <li>Clear the lever's <code>onSprite</code> field so it has no sprite to swap to. Does the event still fire? Which of the two is decoration, and which is the machinery?</li>
            <li>Add a second CaveSwitch and stand between them so both are in range. Press E once. What happened — and should one keypress ever flip two switches? Which of the three approaches would prevent it?</li>
            <li>Take the <code>RemoveListener</code> line out of <code>OnDestroy()</code>, then destroy the object your subscriber sits on while the game is running — a temporary <code>Destroy(gameObject)</code> is enough. Now press E. What does the Console say, and can the Inspector's list, which you also never unsubscribed from, produce the same fault? Say why the answer differs between the two roads.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Event design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>The lever already drives three listeners at once and does not know it. Add a fourth of your own. Why is "whoever wants to know, listens" the direction that scales, and what would the lever have needed to know if it had been written the obvious way?</li>
            <li>Make a false wall that opens a secret passage instead of the cave. How much code did that cost you — and what does that number tell you about the value of the event pattern?</li>
            <li>Make the lever refuse to work unless the player holds the axe. Which script gains a conditional — and can you do it with <em>zero</em> changes to <code>Lever.cs</code>? (Hint: think about who is allowed to be a listener.)</li>
            <li>The lever is shouting into an empty room until someone subscribes. What happens if you wire a listener in the Inspector, then delete the object it points at? What does Unity show you in the slot — and how does that answer differ from deleting an object that subscribed in code, where nothing is shown to you at all?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain a UnityEvent as cause-and-effect with a deliberate gap: the lever never names the door</li>
            <li>Wire a listener in the Inspector, and change the game's rules with no code at all</li>
            <li>Compare three ways to detect "the player is near a lever", and defend yours</li>
            <li>Explain why <code>Interact()</code> must be public — and why <code>onLeverOn</code> being private is the one line of somebody else's file this lesson changes, and what changing it costs</li>
            <li>Argue where an item-gate check belongs, and say what a listener that outlives the object that added it does</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

